            "Start by looking: lid position ([[ptosis|ptosis]]), pupil sizes ([[anisocoria|anisocoria]]), and head posture. Then ask about double vision before you test anything.\n\n[[visual-acuity|Visual acuity]] comes first, before any bright light. Test each eye separately, with the patient's distance glasses or through a pinhole, using a distance chart or a near card held at the stated distance. Record the eye, the chart, the correction, and the result: \"right 6/6 (20/20), left 6/18 (20/60) with glasses, left improves to 6/9 with pinhole.\" If the top line cannot be read, record counting fingers, hand movements, or light perception, and at what distance.\n\n[[confrontation-visual-fields|Visual fields by confrontation]] are also tested one eye at a time. Sit at the patient's eye level about an arm's length away. The patient covers one eye and looks at your nose; you test each quadrant by asking them to count fingers or report a moving finger, bringing the target in from outside the field. A red target compared across the vertical midline helps find subtle defects. Record which eye and which quadrants: \"left eye: inferior temporal quadrant missing to finger counting.\"",
