          body:
            "Look at pupil size and shape in dim light, then test the direct and consensual responses with the patient looking at a distant target. If the patient looks at the light, the pupils constrict as part of the near response and the test is spoiled.\n\nThe [[swinging-flashlight-test|swinging flashlight test]] compares the two optic nerves. In a dim room, shine a bright light into one eye for about two seconds, then swing it quickly across the bridge of the nose to the other eye, and back again, several times, watching the pupil that is lit. Normally both pupils stay equally constricted whichever eye is lit. If one optic nerve conducts less light signal, both pupils dilate when the light swings onto that eye: a [[relative-afferent-pupillary-defect|relative afferent pupillary defect]] (RAPD) on that side. The pupils are still equal in size, because both receive the same output from the brainstem.\n\nFundoscopy comes after the pupils, because dilating drops would make the pupil examination impossible. Look at the optic disc: a normal disc has sharp margins, a pink rim, a central cup, and vessels that are visible as they cross the edge. [[optic-disc-swelling|Disc swelling]] blurs and elevates the margin, hides the vessels where they cross it, and fills in the cup. Bilateral disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure is [[papilledema|papilledema]].",
