          body:
            "Test [[smooth-pursuit|smooth pursuit]] by asking the patient to follow a target held about an arm's length away, moving slowly in an H pattern, with the head still. Ask whether the target ever looks double, and in which direction it is worst. Watch for a limited movement, [[nystagmus|nystagmus]], lid changes, and jerky pursuit. Do not take the target to the far extreme of gaze: a few beats of [[end-point-nystagmus|end-point nystagmus]] there are normal and are easily recorded as a sign.\n\nTest [[saccade|saccades]] by asking the patient to look quickly between your nose and a finger held to each side, then above and below. Look for slow, short, or unequal saccades, for example a slow adducting eye in an [[ino|internuclear ophthalmoplegia]].\n\nIf the patient reports [[diplopia|diplopia]], the [[cover-test|cover test]] shows whether the eyes are misaligned. With the patient fixing on a distant target, cover one eye and watch the other, uncovered eye. If it moves to take up fixation, it was deviated before you covered its partner. An eye that moves outward to fixate was turned in ([[esotropia|esotropia]]); an eye that moves inward was turned out. Repeat for the other eye and in different directions of gaze: the misalignment is largest when looking in the direction of the weak muscle's pull.",
