          body:
            "Screen hearing with a [[whispered-voice-test|whispered voice test]]: stand behind the patient so they cannot lip-read, mask the other ear by pressing and rubbing the tragus, breathe out, and whisper a short combination of numbers and letters for the patient to repeat. Test each ear.\n\nIf hearing is reduced, a 512 Hz tuning fork tells you what kind of loss it is. In the [[rinne-test|Rinne test]], place the vibrating fork on the mastoid bone, then hold it just beside the ear canal, and ask which is louder. Air conduction is normally louder than bone conduction. In the [[weber-test|Weber test]], place the fork in the midline of the forehead or vertex and ask where the sound is heard. Normally it is heard in the middle.\n\n[[conductive-hearing-loss|Conductive loss]] (ear canal or middle ear): Rinne shows bone louder than air on the affected side, and Weber is heard in the affected ear. [[sensorineural-hearing-loss|Sensorineural loss]] (cochlea or cochlear nerve): Rinne still shows air louder than bone on both sides, and Weber is heard in the better ear.",
