            mechanism: "A conductive loss blocks air-conducted sound and also blocks the background noise that normally masks bone-conducted sound, so bone conduction seems louder on the affected side. In sensorineural loss both routes reach the same damaged cochlea or nerve, so air stays louder than bone, but the whole signal is weaker on that side.",
            compare: "A positive Rinne (air louder than bone) is normal and also what you find in sensorineural loss, so a positive Rinne alone does not mean normal hearing. Weber lateralizing to the right fits either a right conductive loss or a left sensorineural loss; the Rinne result and the side of the complaint separate them.",
            deeper: "Tuning fork tests detect larger losses better than small ones, and their accuracy varies widely between studies, so formal audiometry is needed when the result will change management. A sudden sensorineural loss needs prompt audiology and ENT assessment, and a one-sided sensorineural loss also needs a structural cause considered.",
